---
import DoubleTorsionSpring from '@/components/diagrams/DoubleTorsionSpring.astro';
import MaterialsTable from '@/components/ui/MaterialsTable.astro';
import { doubleTorsion } from '@/data/products';
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
---

<ProductLayout product={doubleTorsion}>
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <p>
      A double torsion spring is <strong>two torsion bodies wound in opposite directions</strong>
      and joined by a U-shaped centre loop, from one length of wire. One coil is left-hand, the
      other right-hand. The loop sits on the hinge pin or bracket and the legs take the load on each
      side.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the two bodies act in parallel, the rate is about <strong>twice</strong> that of a
      single body with the same wire, mean diameter and number of coils. The hinge sees a balanced
      couple instead of a single sideways pull. That is why lids, latches and switch mechanisms so
      often use this layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a speciality here. Switchgear and MCB parts in the 0.40–2.50 mm wire range are regular
      work. The centre loop is the detail that usually decides the fit: width, inside radius and
      whether the loop is flat or offset.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same materials as our other springs: hard-drawn spring steel, music wire on request, stainless
      302 / 304 / 316, and oil-tempered wire.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="types">
    <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <figure class="type-fig">
        <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
          <DoubleTorsionSpring annotated />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Left-hand coil, right-hand coil, centre loop and two legs.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Variants we see on drawings: a wide centre loop that sits over a long pin; a tight loop
          that clips onto a bracket; legs of equal length; or one short leg and one long one when
          the two sides of the hinge are not the same.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you already have a single torsion spring and it keeps walking off the pin, a double
          torsion with a centre loop is often the cleaner fix — one part, no pair to match.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <MaterialsTable slot="materials" />

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <p>
      Dimension the <strong>centre loop</strong> the way you would a clip: inside width, radius and
      height off the coil axis. Then the two bodies: ID, coils, wire. Then each leg.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the two sides must open to different angles, say so. A photo of the hinge, even without a
      drawing, is enough for a first quote.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="applications">
    <p>
      On this shop floor the usual jobs are{' '}
      <strong>electrical switchgear and MCB mechanisms</strong>, small automotive latches and lids,
      furniture hinges, and instrumentation covers. The same geometry turns up on appliance lids and
      tool handles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agricultural tines and heavy tail-gate springs are often double torsion as well. Those are
      usually above our 2.50 mm wire limit, so we do not take them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .type-fig {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .type-draw {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grid);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
